			"height":920,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"b945d53941ae0ca7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"london rotary club",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7520,
			"y":10280,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"78d56f5886038f05",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏁 genocide washing",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7240,
			"y":10520,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"dc03d4d5b79bb3fd",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"i wrote two parabolas",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1680,
			"y":12160,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"9385802b5150608b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"I started digging in the content mine",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-360,
			"y":11600,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"e14a7245f36b38df",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"And then i wrote two more",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1600,
			"y":12560,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"3b2ea4d56919ce64",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Melanchon 2027 DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":5420,
			"y":1100,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"92aa118063569517",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🗝️ Any source in particular?\n---\nHis writing at the time, but if you want something precise, check out Daniel Defoe's The Review, Vol. VI, No. 101 on November 10, 1709.\nYou can find a few extracts around that time from the man himself by searching online.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3840,
			"y":8521,
			"width":480,
			"height":200
		},
		{
			"id":"839a29e47b87d496",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"the internet and piracy",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3740,
			"y":9621,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"94da80804b693f8a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"old man yelling at the clouds energy\n?? add meme",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4240,
			"y":8981,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"ff888cb0f93683c4",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"⭐ The story of copyright",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4080,
			"y":9062,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"b4b7ec5acc09de30",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"the statute of anne\n\nsuccession turmoil ??",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4060,
			"y":9221,
			"width":260,
			"height":140,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"4abb0e0df2ecc5b6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"centuries of exploitation",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3930,
			"y":9486,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"fabf8f58496f09f3",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"⭐ The story of the Stationeers",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6312,
			"y":9258,
			"width":995,
			"height":707,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"c6af20895de1c73a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What was it before",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4802,
			"y":8831,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"0a424ad760b7b70b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"✝️ The protestant work ethic?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-80,
			"y":8090,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"40eb47c3ef08be5a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"💵 That worldview where money is a measure of salute?\n---\nMoney really is the ultimate signifier...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":180,
			"y":8200,
			"width":440,
			"height":140,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"7099e851dc27ad23",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the history of protestantism is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":400,
			"y":8420,
			"width":260,
			"height":120,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"f3f817ca4d964d3a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"how was it received",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4060,
			"y":8868,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"3df206418f34d8a5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"So it usually goes with revolutions...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4586,
			"y":672,
			"width":400,
			"height":60,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"1364f5e6e7a345df",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"So Daniel Defoe grew up in a cultish microcosm? \n---\nHe did live in rebel, dissentious districts. ",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4396,
			"y":3053,
			"width":360,
			"height":160,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"ef60b9e37b51d7da",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"So far\n---\nBut people learn...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4386,
			"y":812,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"cc2c941236c1540a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Do I smell the distinctive odor of business school?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4376,
			"y":1512,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"7ed03c78122433b8",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Daniel Defoe received the closest thing to an MBA that existed in the 17th century.\n---\nMathematics and languages as business tools.\nWith a heavy dose of religious and ethical leaning.\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4706,
			"y":1722,
			"width":340,
			"height":260
		},
		{
			"id":"31793240f96b9314",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"It's called \"networking\"\n---\nThe ruling class helping their owns.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4296,
			"y":1742,
			"width":370,
			"height":110,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"4c31c73f1f9710ad",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Wealthy bourgeois merchants, religious extremists, fallen from power...\n---\nAnd hungry for revenge?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4446,
			"y":2547,
			"width":460,
			"height":150,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"531817bb5f8b90ca",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Dignity?\n---\nAs opposed to the \"decadent\" birthright aristocracy.\nToo complacent.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4196,
			"y":2813,
			"width":320,
			"height":160,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"9545fa71db756f1a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"They taught that the \"Flow of Goods\" was God’s way of bringing the world together.\n---\nProperty Rights and Contract Law were nearly as sacred as the Ten Commandments.\n\nThis gave Defoe a lifelong obsession with the \"Dignity of Trade.\"",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4276,
			"y":2062,
			"width":360,
			"height":320,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"9e51b34e90f4962c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Newington Green is a district in the North of London\n---\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newington_Green",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5676,
			"y":1192,
			"width":430,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"894d8e9bcd6407da",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What the *** is a green?\n---\nYou know? The <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Village_green\" target=\"_blank\">greens</a>! From the inclosures!",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5326,
			"y":2232,
			"width":420,
			"height":120,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"4e956b014fce2d23",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the Inclosure story is another DLC.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5216,
			"y":2432,
			"width":200,
			"height":133,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"adc2ce5c16579335",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚰 I hope you have a source for that\n---\nPeter Earle and Maximillian E. Novak",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3926,
			"y":2562,
			"width":380,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"e1aff5886c20bec7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The school was shut down due to religious pressure when Defoe was a young adult.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5286,
			"y":1082,
			"width":420,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"061ca5ebd5e9bf0f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Charles Morton\n---\nA pioneer intellectual who revolutionized education.\nHe ran a private academy for 20 years before moving to America.\nHe went on to have a major role in the founding of Harvard University.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5606,
			"y":1432,
			"width":360,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"fec336acff3f8ceb",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What's a Dissenter?\n---\nAs opposed to Conformists, Dissenters are catholic protestants who refuse the English monarch as \"pope\".\nA lot of them were in some ways refugees from the continent fleeing persecution.\nSome were of French or Flemish origins.\nThey were the religious movement underpinning the republican revolution a decade earlier, and the puritan dictatorship that succeeded.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4786,
			"y":2142,
			"width":420,
			"height":360,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"fd8affe066a584ff",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What happened to them after the return of the monarchy?\n---\nThey had to keep their faith secret since it was illegal.\nThousands fled to America. Most stayed.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4796,
			"y":2753,
			"width":420,
			"height":180,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"f9a56dfc6bb04b2e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The best education money could buy?\n---\nBack in those days, the standard prestigious institutions like Oxford or Cambridge required religious oath to the English religion, which some, like Daniel Defoe's family refused to take.\n\nInstead, they formed privately financed and operated so-called \"Dissenter\" academies, private boarding school which rapidly surpassed the old establishments in the technologies they could afford.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4816,
			"y":1082,
			"width":420,
			"height":360,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"239181232a3195da",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"📍 Does the school building remain?\n---\nThe building did not survive the test of time, but \n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?client=firefox-b-d&q=Charles+Morton+Court\" target=\"_blank\">Charles Morton Court Prestige Student Housing</a> serve as a testament to its legacy.\nIn 2026, it seems to stand above a \"Papa Johns\" and a \"Tesco Express\".\n<a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Prestige+Student+Living+Charles+Morton+Court/@51.5527125,-0.0872473,3a,75y,208.91h,112.2t/data=!3m7!1e1!3m5!1sO7GHLRfen4fRk_ZkWiYi4A!2e0!6shttps:%2F%2Fstreetviewpixels-pa.googleapis.com%2Fv1%2Fthumbnail%3Fcb_client%3Dmaps_sv.tactile%26w%3D900%26h%3D600%26pitch%3D-22.199785617299767%26panoid%3DO7GHLRfen4fRk_ZkWiYi4A%26yaw%3D208.90594074437794!7i16384!8i8192!4m6!3m5!1s0x48761c86b08c46fb:0xd1ae95b42a8e4ec0!8m2!3d51.5526405!4d-0.0873502!16s%2Fg%2F1vvr5q51?entry=ttu&g_ep=EgoyMDI2MDQyOS4wIKXMDSoASAFQAw%3D%3D\" target=\"_blank\">Here is Street View and Google Maps reviews.</a>\n<iframe src=\"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Prestige+Student+Living+Charles+Morton+Court/@51.5527125,-0.0872473,3a,75y,208.91h,112.2t/data=!3m7!1e1!3m5!1sO7GHLRfen4fRk_ZkWiYi4A!2e0!6shttps:%2F%2Fstreetviewpixels-pa.googleapis.com%2Fv1%2Fthumbnail%3Fcb_client%3Dmaps_sv.tactile%26w%3D900%26h%3D600%26pitch%3D-22.199785617299767%26panoid%3DO7GHLRfen4fRk_ZkWiYi4A%26yaw%3D208.90594074437794!7i16384!8i8192!4m6!3m5!1s0x48761c86b08c46fb:0xd1ae95b42a8e4ec0!8m2!3d51.5526405!4d-0.0873502!16s%2Fg%2F1vvr5q51?entry=ttu&g_ep=EgoyMDI2MDQyOS4wIKXMDSoASAFQAw%3D%3D\"></iframe>",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5336,
			"y":1702,
			"width":400,
			"height":440,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"473213a9261ef550",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"A famous religious and intellectual microcosm for Dissenters.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5646,
			"y":1952,
			"width":260,
			"height":190
		},
		{"id":"d3b4516848b37f0d","type":"text","text":"⏰ Daniel Defoe was born in 1660\n---\nIt was a time of renewal, as Charles II restored the monarchy after a brief attempt at a progressive republic that devolved into a puritan military dictatorship.","x":-5486,"y":232,"width":460,"height":200},
		{
			"id":"61c6472d86377400",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"📷 This guy?\n---\nYou can see it for yourself on <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daniel_Defoe_Kneller_Style.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Wikimedia.\n<img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Daniel_Defoe_Kneller_Style.jpg/960px-Daniel_Defoe_Kneller_Style.jpg\" /></a>",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6001,
			"y":390,
			"width":260,
			"height":400
		},
		{
			"id":"5b4d451a709f0da1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Daniel Defoe's family were London candle makers.\n---\nA successful bourgeois family.\nHis father was the first born in England from a puritan Flemish family fleeing religious persecution.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5496,
			"y":602,
			"width":480,
			"height":200
		},
		{"id":"d674c3e21ca88716","type":"text","text":"⭐ The Life of Daniel Defoe\n---\nIn a very condensed format.","x":-5101,"y":-284,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"0"},
		{
			"id":"ecbf5cec60c58bf2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The True-Born Englishman was way more successful than the original poem it was responding to.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4128,
			"y":6574,
			"width":265,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"9cb46f16a0ebdfb7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In fact, the True-Born Englishman was a response...\n---\nIt was mostly a response to John Tutchin's 1700 poem \"The Foreigners\", attacking the Dutch ties of the king.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3803,
			"y":6574,
			"width":360,
			"height":180,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"1eee3e89560bf812",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 John Tutchin\n\nA radical political journalist. \nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Tutchin",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3988,
			"y":6814,
			"width":260,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"c0925e9d6778c0a2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚰 John Tutchin's \"The Foreigners\"\n---\nOn the <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1641-1700_the-foreigners-a-poem-_tutchin-john_1700/page/4/mode/2up\">Internet Archive</a>\n<iframe src=\"https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1641-1700_the-foreigners-a-poem-_tutchin-john_1700/page/4/mode/2up\"></iframe>",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3688,
			"y":6814,
			"width":265,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"8e055b600367b97e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In essence, a pro-immigration plea?\n---\nYes,  mostly focused on calling racial purity a myth, and pointing out shameful origins of established aristocrats.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4208,
			"y":6009,
			"width":350,
			"height":213
		},
		{
			"id":"15ffce56be6fb0f6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":" 🚰 The True-Born Englishman?\n ---\n You can read the full text on <a href=\"https://gutenberg.org/ebooks/30159\">Project Gutenberg</a>.\n \n <iframe src=\"https://gutenberg.org/ebooks/30159\"></iframe>",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3848,
			"y":6153,
			"width":340,
			"height":283
		},
		{
			"id":"ebf764b1093a8127",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"I do like this quote:\n---\nA true-born Englishman's a contradiction,  \nIn speech an irony, in fact a fiction;",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4163,
			"y":6257,
			"width":260,
			"height":230,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"5b9234d08f40366a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🗝️ In 1701, Daniel Defoe published The True-Born Englishman.\n---\nAt 41, he published this satirical poem against the xenophobic culture of England of the time.\nIt was aimed at defending the dutch born king, William III.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4508,
			"y":5637,
			"width":400,
			"height":280,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"05a22844cd0ac569",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Daniel Defoe's True-Born Englishman was a massive hit.\n---\nFirst published anonymously, the demand for it grew quickly, and many unauthorized copies were produced.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4788,
			"y":6070,
			"width":480,
			"height":187,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"8725a0235c1a2659",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Daniel Defoe was quite upset that his poem had been widely copied.\n---\nIt appears that the majority of copies in circulation were cheap unauthorized versions on which he did not make any profit.\nHe claimed that up to 80000 copies were circulated, a staggering amount for the time.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4768,
			"y":6397,
			"width":460,
			"height":232,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"4ee260ef31d9900b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"\"France?\" What does France have to do with it?\n---\nDefoe was a fervent admirer of Louis XIV's militaristic centralism.\nBy talking about France, he could criticize the English government with a veneer of deniability.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5490,
			"y":6553,
			"width":464,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"bbebbd90befb593a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He really did like allegories...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5204,
			"y":6864,
			"width":178,
			"height":77
		},
		{
			"id":"4aa765f37fcebc89",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, Louis XIV is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5430,
			"y":6878,
			"width":200,
			"height":133,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"09813e983375b960",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Queen Anne\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anne,_Queen_of_Great_Britain",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6775,
			"y":5944,
			"width":225,
			"height":129
		},
		{
			"id":"9f04098fd76affe4",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 Robert Harley",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6095,
			"y":7074,
			"width":180,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"d558c210ac6f1acc",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚰 Daniel Defoe's \"The Shortest-Way with the Dissenter\"\n---\nOn the <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/shortest-way-dissenters\">Internet Archive</a>\n<iframe src=\"https://archive.org/details/shortest-way-dissenters\"></iframe>",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6605,
			"y":6418,
			"width":490,
			"height":271
		},
		{
			"id":"892f81aa580d4be9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the Glorious Revolution is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6963,
			"y":5589,
			"width":260,
			"height":115,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"0fa336742d48d5a5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"👥 King William III\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_III_of_England",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6663,
			"y":5746,
			"width":225,
			"height":129
		},
		{
			"id":"674e6a3c3e3e61d0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In 1703, Daniel Defoe was imprisoned for a satirical poem.\n---\nHe had published \"The Shortest-Way with the Dissenters\" anonymously but was later found out.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6745,
			"y":6148,
			"width":400,
			"height":220,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"516d8b49569ae19a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The Shortest-Way with the Dissenters?\n---\nIt was written from the point of view of a bloodthirsty catholic extremist.\nDaniel Defoe essentially impersonated his opponents and exaggerated their positions.\nThey initially loved it, and many believed that the government was actually planning a massacre.\nWhen the truth came out, everyone was understanbly quite upset.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7040,
			"y":6418,
			"width":360,
			"height":390,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"939fb96ab11834bf",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The new king was obviously quite controversial.\n---\nDaniel Defoe defended him in a satyrical poem.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6488,
			"y":5277,
			"width":505,
			"height":134,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"a8d38e0524f7896d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What kind of government?\n---\nThe politics at the time were an intense war between the \"progressive\" Whigs and the \"traditionalist\" Tories.\nThe balance of power was a constant tug-of-war.\nDaniel Defoe's positions were closer to the Whigs, but he was spying for the Tories.\nAs a result, he was widely distrusted and seen as a double agent.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6870,
			"y":7074,
			"width":470,
			"height":252,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"be08d3a2e0fb21b3",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"King William III died in 1702, his wife, Queen Anne, succeeded him to the throne.\n---\nShe was not close with Daniel Defoe. In fact she had strong ties to the Church of England.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6365,
			"y":5732,
			"width":340,
			"height":268,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"20daebf4571d973f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In Prison, he started his own private publication.\n---\nA newsletter if you will.\n<a href=\"https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_El8TAAAAQAAJ\" target=\"_blank\">\"A Review of the Affairs of France\"</a> started as a weekly chronicle that shifted according to Daniel Defoe's interests.\n<iframe src=\"https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_El8TAAAAQAAJ\"></iframe>",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6055,
			"y":6258,
			"width":460,
			"height":267,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"a331d00687a688d3",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Though the rest of his life would be full of political intrigue...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5903,
			"y":4210,
			"width":420,
			"height":110,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"f9a85ef1f4484755",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What kind of political intrigue?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6053,
			"y":4453,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"4a3fa30454592086",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"At 25, Daniel Defoe took part in the Monmouth Rebellion\n---\nA protestant failed rebellion against the catholic king.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6398,
			"y":4546,
			"width":260,
			"height":260,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"d1a142f0a782dd56",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the Monmouth Rebellion is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6183,
			"y":4920,
			"width":260,
			"height":115,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"041706c4eac2b33b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Very much a rebel entrepreneur of the time.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6408,
			"y":4288,
			"width":260,
			"height":55
		},
		{
			"id":"27a1954a4a5e8183",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"With deep ties to politics...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6660,
			"y":4423,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"db9af5deecdd4403",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Wow, what happened to him then?\n---\nDaniel Defoe received a pardon and continued business as usual.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6768,
			"y":4849,
			"width":360,
			"height":161
		},
		{
			"id":"19a8c144da17f00b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Untill the Glorious Revolution of 1688\n---\nA group of protestant elites worried about the catholic king organized a rebellion.\nIt worked and the king's son-in-law, a protestant, acceded to the throne.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6800,
			"y":5037,
			"width":500,
			"height":201,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"bda37b01f524fff7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Glorious?\n---\nHistory is written by the winners.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6963,
			"y":5436,
			"width":260,
			"height":120,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"8676648ae26bcd81",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"💼 During his early life, Daniel Defoe was a travelling salesman.\n---\nHe rode thousands of miles all across England and Scotland.\nHe dealt in stockings (wool, silk, and cotton), but also branched out into wine, spirits, and tobacco.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4685,
			"y":3709,
			"width":470,
			"height":210,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"82457e74613347a6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚢 Travel, you say?\n---\nThere is no historical record of Daniel Defoe ever setting foot outside the British Isles.\nThere are however a lot of mentions of him being an avid armchair traveler.\nHe would read everything he came across when it came to international travel.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4440,
			"y":3969,
			"width":300,
			"height":320,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"3c15c4f2632c1045",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"So... the author of Robinson Crusoe had no actual travel experience?\n---\nWell, it would appear so.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4015,
			"y":4347,
			"width":260,
			"height":185,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"6a3eb1ddad2c0ac6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Did Daniel Defoe read any fiction?\n---\nHe was famously suspicious of fiction, that his religious upbringing pushed him to consider \"lies told for pleasure\".\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3990,
			"y":4729,
			"width":540,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"458077cf65aea0f1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He loved to gather information.\n---\nThat was the key to a good deal.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4480,
			"y":4777,
			"width":410,
			"height":120,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"c3f78645024410ce",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"I also think it's important to understand the world...\n---\nAnd where things come from.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4480,
			"y":4949,
			"width":420,
			"height":145,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"c06caf1ce1c855b9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Wait, how did he reconcile that with the fact that he literaly wrote fiction?\n---\nDefoe didn’t just ignore the contradiction; he built an entire philosophical defense for it. He called his method \"Religious Allegory\" or \"Parabolic Writing.\"",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3970,
			"y":4964,
			"width":520,
			"height":210,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"6f30d97bbab96763",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What kind of book are we talking about here?\n---\nHe studied the Great Atlases and the journals of merchant travelers of the times.\nHe kept up to date with scientific colonial exploration.\nHe also had an interest for practical knowledge manuals, which would later give his own writing some realism and depth.\nSome names come to mind...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4505,
			"y":4389,
			"width":420,
			"height":330
		},
		{
			"id":"18924d63f3bc5fa5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"💸 They made it!\n---\nThey were \"New Money\".\nThe American dream...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4926,
			"y":812,
			"width":260,
			"height":145,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"21687949a41e78bc",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Daniel Defoe studied in Charles Morton’s Academy at Newington Green\n---\nIt was a brand new kind of education.\nThe best money could buy.\nIt was a very progressive education focused on knowledge for the sake of doing.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5846,
			"y":885,
			"width":480,
			"height":245,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"833cbd5359bf31db",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The king was quite pleased with the way Daniel Defoe defended him\n---\nDaniel Defoe became somewhat of an advisor to him in the last years of his life.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6487,
			"y":5477,
			"width":375,
			"height":224,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"6bf4d15935fded99",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏴‍☠️ Around 1709, Daniel Defoe's \"Review\" turned its attention to the question of piracy\n---\nNo, not the one in the sea.\nUpset about his financial loss of opportunity cost in his previous experiences, he started claiming a bold new idea: a BOOK should be the AUTHOR’s Property.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4550,
			"y":8321,
			"width":390,
			"height":300,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"1fe733323747f354",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"microsoft owns albyon",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2590,
			"y":9920,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"0960ba5e853c2d1b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"⭐ The life of Selkirk\nSelkirk backstory, orange, star",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2050,
			"y":7220,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"f8195e67d0bd73b7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"selkirk story published",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1980,
			"y":7753,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"226ec3fff23eaa94",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏁 end of selkirk",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1710,
			"y":7936,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"5457838724c4f71f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"selirk childhood",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2010,
			"y":7380,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"bbc20a2e28182560",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"⏰ On 2 February 1709\n---\nThe shipwrecked Alexander Selkirk was rescued by the way of Duke.\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2470,
			"y":7520,
			"width":330,
			"height":147,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"9fa40b1636a990f3",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Woodes Rogers",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2140,
			"y":6755,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"e7fb061c2668af1b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"William Dampier",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1640,
			"y":6785,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"e56458bd0df6f92f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"defoe rest of life",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":620,
			"y":7336,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"2e1ab5221c315e9d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏁 end of defoe",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":620,
			"y":7476,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"af6635b4e9d0d251",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"How did it compare to the one in the sea",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4485,
			"y":8821,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"e302cb83277536b7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🧱 Where can I read the primary source?\n---\nAlthough the content of old periodicals are in the public domain by now, only rare digitized extracts are available to the public.\nMost of them are behind the paywall of private publishers like <a href=\"https://www.jstor.org/stable/20779451\" target=\"_blank\">JSTOR</a> or <a href=\"https://www.taylorfrancis.com/books/mono/10.4324/9781003576228/defoe-review-1704-13-volume-6-1709-10-part-john-mcveagh\" target=\"_blank\">Taylor & Francis</a>.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3670,
			"y":8815,
			"width":340,
			"height":226
		},
		{
			"id":"509848ca1071f626",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, The Growing Inaccessibility Of Science is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3590,
			"y":9106,
			"width":260,
			"height":115,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"6d889bbbf0bab92e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"1709 you say?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3160,
			"y":7906,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{"id":"more","type":"text","text":"Could there be MORE to this story?\n---\nCould there be hidden depths burried far below what meets the eye?","x":160,"y":1760,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"more2","type":"text","text":"Could there be an orange button drowned in a sea of grey?\n---\nThat is when the human found a story.\n  It laid there, shining green, in the middle of all the buttons.\n  It was story left untold, almost forgotten.\n  And its title was...","x":160,"y":2020,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"f919133328a5e0d3","type":"text","text":"It was a groundbreaking book\n---\n\nfirst book starting with \"I\"","x":-585,"y":5173,"width":800,"height":620,"color":"1"},
		{
			"id":"15e35a10feb5147c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"true\ntheres a lot to unpack here\n\nclickbait etc rework with the notes",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":385,
			"y":5058,
			"width":400,
			"height":340,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{"id":"e100bf5300059e3b","type":"text","text":"That's a very long title...\n---","x":825,"y":5378,"width":390,"height":305,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"bcc0cef568d3e4b0","type":"text","text":"Wait... written by himself?\n---\n\nhistory of autobiographical fiction\nreview the notes","x":1265,"y":5483,"width":300,"height":355,"color":"1"},
		{
			"id":"b88844f76bf4c415",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏁 and thats the end of robinson crusoe",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-695,
			"y":4689,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"c026b7ef76f5979a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"defoe in 1719",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-900,
			"y":4130,
			"width":630,
			"height":180,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{"id":"e8a4775851bc5327","type":"text","text":"📷 That's not the title of the book, though...\n---\nIts original title was:\n\"The\nLIFE\nand\nStrange Surprizing \nADVENTURES\nof \nRobinson Crusoe\nof York, Mariner:\n\nWho lived Eight and Twenty Years, all alone in an un-inhabited Island on the Coast of America, near the Mouth of the Great River of Oroonoque;\nHaving been cast on Shore by Shipwreck, wherein all the Men perished but himself. \n\nWITH\nAn Account how he was at last as strangely deliver'd by Pyrates. \nWritten by Himself.\"\n\nYou can see it for yourself on <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Robinson_Crusoe_1719_1st_edition.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Wikimedia.\n<img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1e/Robinson_Crusoe_1719_1st_edition.jpg\" /></a>","x":265,"y":3530,"width":440,"height":460,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"d59c4d2f4f4674df","type":"text","text":"Strange surprizing adventures...\n---\nLike the one you're living right now!","x":845,"y":4060,"width":260,"height":140,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"72d5074d19b63713","type":"text","text":"You forgot the part at the bottom...","x":1465,"y":4060,"width":300,"height":80,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"ed26200129d229b8","type":"text","text":"👥 Printed for W. Taylor","x":1415,"y":4200,"width":230,"height":100,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"99677d0088771e0d","type":"text","text":"📍 at the Shop in Pater-Noster-Row","x":1685,"y":4200,"width":200,"height":120,"color":"0"},
		{
			"id":"129c1ea38464bd60",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"I know...\n---\nIt's probably more than they deserve.\nIn fact this is precisely about getting more than one deserves.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":765,
			"y":3400,
			"width":260,
			"height":220,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{"id":"title","type":"text","text":"⭐ The true story of the story of Robinson Crusoe\n---","x":-515,"y":2580,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"title_aside","type":"text","text":"The story of a white man?\n---\nAlas, that is where we must start.\n  Humans do love their stories to be character-centered.\n  But fear not, you will see many characters if you press the right buttons.","x":25,"y":2820,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"49209c0bf5486f14","type":"text","text":"I can't help but wonder if this psychological bias in stories reinforces a hyper-individualistic society.\n---","x":25,"y":3120,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"title_aside2","type":"text","text":"Though I guess a lot of them were white men.\n---\nSorry, its mostly their story.\n  But it doesn't have to be about them.","x":525,"y":3100,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"6a342d3d556dcaa3","type":"text","text":"What do you mean, the story of the story?\n---\nI mean the story of how the book came to be, and the story of its author.","x":-760,"y":2820,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"d0c1f86e10f949da","type":"text","text":"Robinson Crusoe?\n---\nRobinson Crusoe is an English adventure novel by Daniel Defoe, first published on 25 April 1719.\nMost people are familiar with its narrative of a sailor shipwrecked on a wild island.\nBut few people even know the story of the story.","x":-715,"y":3100,"width":300,"height":200,"color":"4"},
		{
			"id":"e1ef83e94426eeac",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"it is widely credited for inventing realism",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-445,
			"y":6088,
			"width":260,
			"height":150,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"732c607729abf65a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Authenticity",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-15,
			"y":6358,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"99af32b09b97fb14",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The history of PR is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":85,
			"y":6538,
			"width":260,
			"height":105,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"a8f5434158d26eeb",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"What the fuck is a parabola\n\nlinkback to beginning",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2780,
			"y":8561,
			"width":580,
			"height":160,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"f033b3b0f313a483",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"After the Union Act, Daniel Defoe spent a lot of time in Edinburgh spying for the government.\n---\nHe monitored political dissent on the ground.\nAs the Union settled, he turned his attention to domestic affairs.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5890,
			"y":7011,
			"width":390,
			"height":297,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"c78c58970d985108",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🚧 I'm sorry, the Act of Union is in another DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5657,
			"y":6848,
			"width":200,
			"height":133,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"5929c71a78c15ca1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Daniel Defoe and his \"review\" were instrumental in the redacting of the Act of Union in 1707\n---\nYou know, the creation of a \"United Kingdom\".\nBut this is a story for another DLC.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5915,
			"y":6629,
			"width":398,
			"height":180,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"10818a63bbc83eb9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Prison?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6185,
			"y":6713,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"f2c4a78c2f9975be",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Until he became a spy for the government!\n---\nThe Tory minister Robert Harley bailed him out of prison on condition that Daniel Defoe work for him.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6590,
			"y":6800,
			"width":380,
			"height":230,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"53e72cec6b7fa897",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Spying?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6115,
			"y":6855,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"7be6ad5f9bf661ad",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"i genuinely thought defoe would have interesting travel and piracy stories\n\ni dug into the story of defoe",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1380,
			"y":11220,
			"width":540,
			"height":180,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"36627db47b08c47e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"mb connect back to mining colonial exploitation into minecraft DLC",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6074,
			"y":10320,
			"width":260,
			"height":160,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"5802689e88e00d7a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"parabolas like",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1940,
			"y":12820,
			"width":300,
			"height":140,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"12287a691a73d028",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Do you want to hear it?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8200,
			"y":9028,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"e97a0502123e630c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The Human was puzzled by this weird tale.\n---\nIt was very different from most of the Content the Human had seen.\nWhat color was it, even?\nWhat did it mean?\nThe Human would have to think about it some more.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8130,
			"y":10348,
			"width":560,
			"height":200,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"24381438ebd2ccb0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The Human looked around, and pondered for a while. \n---\nDeep, deep, down the Content Mines were billions of little pebbles, each a slightly different color.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8180,
			"y":10608,
			"width":460,
			"height":170,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"15980fbc13bb0c44",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏁 And for a brief moment, the Human would behold.\n---\nAnd the pebble would shine, illuminated by their torch.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8223,
			"y":11648,
			"width":375,
			"height":160,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"83971dce8d62c4e6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":" Most of them too small to ever matter. Most of them weird and worthless.\n ---\n Could one be a color nobody saw before?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8180,
			"y":10828,
			"width":460,
			"height":180,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"14edf248898aec8e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"But the Human had been promised.\n---\nDeep, deep down there was were true treasures lie.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8180,
			"y":11068,
			"width":460,
			"height":120,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"83c0899d0ee219f2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"So the Human kept digging.\n---\nDug further, deeper than anyone before.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8180,
			"y":11248,
			"width":460,
			"height":120,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"c193f89345ffcbe2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"And every now and then, the Human would stop and look at a pebble.\n---\nA pebble that the light of day would probably never reach.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8155,
			"y":11428,
			"width":510,
			"height":140,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"0abd418c5bf0aee6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The eyes sent a signal to the brain. It remained as a memory long after the light went out.\n---\n\"These Neurons are Mine!\" the boy cried, and most of them agreed.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9680,
			"y":9748,
			"width":440,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"9471b87102b37668",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He saved his PNG to JPG. He even saved to TXT! He made new formats and new programs so that his Drawing corresponded to many numbers and many many texts.\n---\n\"These Numbers are Mine!\" the boy cried, and most of them agreed.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9670,
			"y":10168,
			"width":460,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"fc8f039d92d1418e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He took the fourth Drawing and changed the color.\n---\n\"This Drawing is Mine!\" the boy cried, and most of them agreed.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9100,
			"y":10488,
			"width":340,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"9641b2aebd8ad4b7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"As for the last Drawing...",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9700,
			"y":11148,
			"width":240,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"edf278694719a26c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He scanned the third Drawing into a computer. It made a PNG, a binary number on a silicon chip.\n---\n\"This Number is Mine!\" the boy cried, and most of them agreed.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9170,
			"y":10088,
			"width":380,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"0fd10a82bb365399",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He took the first Drawing and cut a small part, showing just a few colored shapes.\n---\n\"These Shapes are Mine!\" the boy cried, and most of them agreed.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9200,
			"y":9308,
			"width":400,
			"height":200
		},
		{
			"id":"599163474d3b9662",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"On the other side of the world, a girl made a drawing of herself near a house.\nShe didn't know about the boy who owned all the Drawings.\nShe didn't know anything, she was too young to even speak.\nBut her drawing happened to be exactly identical to the last Drawing.\n---\n\"This drawing is Mine!\" the boy cried, and most of them agreed.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9510,
			"y":11288,
			"width":620,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"ae2485302e589cc1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏁 For her shameless theft, the girl was thrown into a jail, surrounded by Mines in the sand.\nThe boy continued to own all the Drawings.\nWhen people wanted new Drawings, they came to see him, and he would sell them one.\nBut he would always say as he gave them away:\n---\n\"This Drawing is Mine!\" and most of them agreed.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9510,
			"y":11548,
			"width":620,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"a4f5abadbd2bcfee",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He drew over it more and more, erased here, added traits there, so that the Drawing became identical to a famous Drawing.\n---\n\"This Drawing is Mine!\" the boy cried, and most of them agreed.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9600,
			"y":10568,
			"width":460,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"d758051e241964bb",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He drew over some more and it became another. Some more still and yet another.\n---\n\"All the Drawings are Mine!\" the boy cried, and most of them agreed.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9600,
			"y":10848,
			"width":460,
			"height":180
		},
		{"id":"0d5855d070abe7b6","type":"text","text":"⭐ The boy who owned all the drawings","x":9160,"y":8788,"width":480,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{
			"id":"24240e64634959a5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He showed the second Drawing to people. The light reflected the Drawing on their eyes.\n---\n\"These Eyes are Mine!\" the boy cried, and most of them agreed.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9160,
			"y":9688,
			"width":420,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"249743e9d7aff4d6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"There once was a boy, no older than a child, who made five Drawings of himself near a Mine.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9190,
			"y":8888,
			"width":420,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"bcbd0525ad1e316c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He lived in a world where Everything was Property.\n---\n\"These Drawings are Mine!\" the boy cried, and most of them agreed.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9190,
			"y":9008,
			"width":420,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"8e088360bd57291f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"He cut his Drawing even more, so that it was just a colorful square.\n---\n\"This Color is Mine!\" the boy cried, and most of them agreed.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9660,
			"y":9428,
			"width":440,
			"height":160
		},
		{"id":"19e5fd7f23f8fd04","type":"text","text":"⭐ This is the story of a human.\n---\nA human who mined content in the content mines.\nA human like any other.","x":8120,"y":7629,"width":480,"height":115,"color":"6"},
		{
			"id":"8da47f57273fe7c8",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Sometimes it was Blue!\n---\nRarer Content even!",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8180,
			"y":7988,
			"width":360,
			"height":100,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"d1e1705298e2c5e8",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"And every now and then, the Human found really meaningful, life-changing Content!\n---\nIt would be Purple, or even Orange!",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8100,
			"y":8153,
			"width":520,
			"height":175,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"b48c1a929392d1f5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Might there be other colors of Content, deeper down the Mine? \n---\nPerhaps even some color nobody had ever seen? ",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8120,
			"y":8408,
			"width":480,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"e6d90edba5127fbb",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"One day, the Human found a weird tale, deep at the bottom of the Content Mine.\n---\nIt was a tale written by a Human and it was called \"The boy who owned all the drawings\".",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8120,
			"y":8708,
			"width":420,
			"height":200
		},
		{
			"id":"2f238287f04d2a46",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Sometimes the Content was slightly Green.\n---\nThat meant it was slightly rare.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8190,
			"y":7788,
			"width":340,
			"height":130,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"6eca2001d25b0b72",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Every day, every night, the Human mined and mined.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8230,
			"y":8586,
			"width":260,
			"height":55
		},
		{
			"id":"91ce07fab199b5d0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The Human did not hear the tale.\n---\nThe Human kept digging. Dug further, deeper than anyone before.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8020,
			"y":9308,
			"width":660,
			"height":120,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"d8e34c627353ef9b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Eager to get a promised treasure, the Person dug and dug some more.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8140,
			"y":9478,
			"width":420,
			"height":90,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"ca368645279d5073",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Surrounded by pebbles, deeper, always deeper, the Person dug some more.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8180,
			"y":9688,
			"width":340,
			"height":80,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"9d59fd903533cb04",
			"type":"text",
			"text":">> mined attention ?? mb stick to shallow easy work ?\n== an interactive visual novel hommage to Curiosity: What's Inside the Cube? and The Cyberiad\n\n\t* Welcome to this new innovative game engine combining the elements of visual novel and clicker.\n\t* Sorry about the art, I'm not good at drawing. Please forgive me, for reasons that will become very clear very soon this game is only available in English. You are welcome to translate it with whatever tool you want. BTW This was made entirely without AI, whatever that might mean for you.\n\t* Click on the Content Mine to find Content. Once you have enough Content, you can turn to the next page of the book, next level of the mine. The book keeps you company while you Mine, the Mine gives you reward while you dig deeper. Win/win.\n\t* And of course, a pleasant progression and big climax at the end!\n\n---\n* On November 6th, 2012, Apple released accidentally \"Curiosity: What's Inside the Cube?\", an experimental game from Peter Molyneux intended to release at 12:22 on November 7th.\n* The game was simple: a cube with a mystery inside. It would open after 69 billion clicks. Only the last clicker would see and get the content of the cube.\n* On May 26th, 2013, after almost 7 months of collaborative clicking, Peter Molyneux announced the prize to everyone: the winner was to be a god in their next game, Godus.\n* In a 2013 Wired magazine, Molyneux indicated that the monetization options offered in the game to increase clicking rate generated \"a few tens of thousands\".\n* It is an interesting sum, small enough to barely register for his company, but big enough to really impact an individual life.\n* By the way it's well known in the psychology of game design that in monetization schemes like in many social phenomena following Pareto's law, 80% of the revenue is generated by 20% of the people. You know, the 99%/1% split.\n* That's microgaming whales, that's billionaire oligarch, that's every social network reproducing what's called a \"scale-free\" structure.\n---\n* The person who did the last click was named Bryan. He was promised the title of \"God of God\" in Godus, and a 1% share of its revenue.\n* Godus never left early access, Bryan received neither the economical ownership nor the metaphorical one he was promised over its world.\n* Peter Molyneux moved on to other projects. In 2026, he released in early access a project touted as his last. He claims it takes place in the same universe as Fable, but this cannot be explicit because Microsoft now owns the world he created.\n* He named the game \"Albion\", claiming that a historical term cannot be copyrighted.\n---\n* \"Curiosity: What's Inside the Cube?\" will forever remain as a weird experiment at the border between capitalism and poetry.\n* Its beautiful rendition of the prisoner dilemma at scale complemented a crisp allegory of the chase for object petit a, a lacanian theory best explained by philosopher Slavoj Zizek.\n* By the way, Lacan was notoriously right wing. His theory doesn't really address notions such as competition, or systemic phenomena for that matter.\n* Since this game was released, many more billions of clicks have been hammered down the Content Mines.\n* Clicks from Creators, working hard on their crafts.\n* Clicks from their Supporters, each platform taking a cut.\n* Clicks from Consumers, watching among the ads.\n* Clicks from Everyone! Who can Exist without Content?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":7630,
			"y":6168,
			"width":1200,
			"height":1060,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"835d6f6466cb8676",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The minecraft DLC gameplay DLC is probably an everlasting DLC (torch scanning)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":6560,
			"y":9248,
			"width":260,
			"height":140,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"82e620a3e84fed09",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"🏁 end of minecraft dlc\n(mb mention that the cube kept growing towards the end)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":6560,
			"y":10148,
			"width":380,
			"height":160,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
